<% locals.currentPage = 'about'; %>
<%- include('../partials/header') %>

<section class="tk-hero--tight" style="padding-bottom:28px">
  <div class="tk-breadcrumb">// HOME / ABOUT</div>
  <h1 class="tk-h1">
    About TronKits.<br>
    <span class="tk-h1__sub">One tinkerer, one Raspberry Pi, a drawer full of calculators.</span>
  </h1>
</section>

<!-- ============================== WHO I AM =============================== -->
<section class="tk-section-tight">
  <div class="tk-section-head">
    <div class="tk-section-head__n">01</div>
    <div class="tk-section-head__body">
      <div class="tk-section-head__kicker">// Who I am</div>
      <h2 class="tk-h2">George Clay</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="tk-section-head__rule"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tk-prose" style="margin:0">
    <!-- TODO(george): add a photo here if you want one (e.g. /images/george.jpg, ~400px square). -->
    <p>
      I'm George Clay, and I build TronKits. I'm a self-taught tinkerer: I learn by
      building something, breaking it, and writing down what fixed it. This site is where
      those notes and the little tools I kept rebuilding for myself end up.
    </p>
    <!-- TODO(george): one or two sentences of background — what you do (or did) for a living, and how long you've been at the bench. Kept generic on purpose until you fill it in. -->
    <p>
      I first learned the resistor color code
      <a href="/blog/reading-resistor-color-code">in the Navy</a>, and I still keep a printed
      color-code chart taped to the wall behind my workbench. These days most of my
      projects are a mix of electronics, 3D-printed enclosures designed in OpenSCAD, and
      small web apps.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since early 2026 I've been building a lot with AI. I use Claude, ChatGPT and Gemini
      as a <a href="/blog/building-a-team-with-multiple-ai-models">team</a> &mdash; passing a
      design from one model to the next and asking each what is missing &mdash; and that
      process took one idea, an AI-driven hog trap, all the way to a provisional patent
      application for a distributed edge-AI capture system. I wrote up the good and the bad
      of that in <a href="/blog/journey-into-ai">The Journey Into AI</a>.
    </p>
    <!-- TODO(george): where you're based, if you want it public. -->
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ========================== WHAT THIS SITE IS ========================== -->
<section class="tk-section">
  <div class="tk-section-head">
    <div class="tk-section-head__n">02</div>
    <div class="tk-section-head__body">
      <div class="tk-section-head__kicker">// What this site is</div>
      <h2 class="tk-h2">A workbench, not a magazine</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="tk-section-head__rule"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tk-2col">
    <div class="tk-card tk-card--flat">
      <div class="tk-kicker tk-mb-18">// WHAT'S HERE</div>
      <ul class="tk-body" style="font-size:15px; padding-left:18px; margin:0">
        <li style="margin-bottom:8px"><strong>Electronics calculators</strong> &mdash;
          <a href="/resistor">resistor color code</a>, <a href="/555">555 timer</a>,
          <a href="/voltage-divider">voltage divider</a>, <a href="/led-resistor">LED resistor</a>
          and <a href="/ohms-law">Ohm's law</a>.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:8px"><strong>An <a href="/scad">OpenSCAD box generator</a></strong>
          that writes the code for a parametric box and can render it to an STL.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:8px"><strong><a href="/tutorials">Tutorials</a></strong> &mdash;
          OpenSCAD boxes, and setting up a Raspberry Pi as a Node.js web server.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:8px"><strong>Small dev utilities</strong> &mdash;
          <a href="/csv-viewer">CSV viewer</a>, <a href="/csv2app">CSV2App</a>,
          <a href="/passphrases">passphrase generator</a>,
          <a href="/logo-generator">logo generator</a> and a
          <a href="/project-tracker">project tracker</a>.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/blog">Field notes</a></strong> &mdash; write-ups on the tools,
          the electronics behind them, and working with AI.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="tk-card tk-card--flat">
      <div class="tk-kicker tk-mb-18">// WHERE IT RUNS</div>
      <p class="tk-body" style="font-size:15px; margin-bottom:12px">
        TronKits is served from a Raspberry Pi sitting in a colocation rack. It runs a
        Node.js (Express) app, kept alive by PM2, behind an Nginx reverse proxy, with a UFW
        firewall in front. OpenSCAD is installed on the same Pi for STL rendering.
      </p>
      <p class="tk-body" style="font-size:15px; margin-bottom:0">
        The whole setup, from SD card to static IP, is written up step by step in the
        <a href="/tutorials/raspberry-pi-node-nginx-colocation">Raspberry Pi colocation tutorial</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- =========================== HOW TOOLS WORK ============================ -->
<section class="tk-section">
  <div class="tk-section-head">
    <div class="tk-section-head__n">03</div>
    <div class="tk-section-head__body">
      <div class="tk-section-head__kicker">// How the tools work</div>
      <h2 class="tk-h2">Free, no login, and where your data goes</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="tk-section-head__rule"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tk-prose" style="margin:0 0 24px">
    <p>
      Every tool is free and none of them need an account. Most run entirely in your
      browser, so nothing you type or load leaves your machine. Two need the server, and
      they're marked below so you know exactly what is sent.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="tk-card" style="padding:0; overflow-x:auto">
    <table class="tk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Runs</th><th>What reaches the server</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Resistor, 555, divider, LED, Ohm's law</td><td>Browser</td><td>Nothing &mdash; the maths is JavaScript on the page</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CSV Viewer, CSV2App</td><td>Browser</td><td>Nothing &mdash; your file is parsed locally and never uploaded</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Logo generator</td><td>Browser</td><td>Nothing &mdash; the SVG is built on the page</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Project tracker</td><td>Browser</td><td>Nothing &mdash; data stays in local storage or a JSON file you pick</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Passphrase generator</td><td>Server</td><td>Only the length, count and exact-length setting; passphrases are generated by a script on the Pi and returned, not saved</td></tr>
        <tr><td>OpenSCAD box &rarr; STL</td><td>Server</td><td>The OpenSCAD code; the Pi renders it with OpenSCAD and saves the <code>.scad</code> and <code>.stl</code> files so you can download them</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Contact form</td><td>Server</td><td>Your name, email and message, stored so I can reply</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="tk-body" style="font-size:14px; margin-top:18px; max-width:760px">
    Like most web servers, this one keeps a plain access log: the time, IP address and URL
    of each request. There are no ads and no analytics scripts.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- ============================== AI POLICY ============================== -->
<section class="tk-section">
  <div class="tk-section-head">
    <div class="tk-section-head__n">04</div>
    <div class="tk-section-head__body">
      <div class="tk-section-head__kicker">// Who wrote what</div>
      <h2 class="tk-h2">How I use AI on this site</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="tk-section-head__rule"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tk-prose" style="margin:0">
    <p>
      I write some posts and tutorials myself and have AI write others. I'm not hiding
      either. For the AI-written ones, Claude works from a voice file built from my own
      writing, I edit the result, strip out the obvious AI tells, and make sure I agree with
      every claim before it goes up. The long version is in
      <a href="/blog/ai-slop">AI Slop?</a>
    </p>
    <p>Every post from here on ends with one of two bylines, so you always know:</p>
    <blockquote><em>I, George Clay, wrote this post in whole. AI was used to clean up my grammar/spelling, and point out any gaps in continuity.</em></blockquote>
    <blockquote><em>This blog post was written by AI using the voice of George Clay's writings. Edited by George Clay.</em></blockquote>
    <p>
      Some older posts predate the rule and have no byline. Much of the site's code was
      written with Claude Code, too.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="tk-section-tight">
  <div class="tk-cta-strip">
    <div style="flex:1">
      <div class="tk-cta-strip__kicker">// GET IN TOUCH</div>
      <h3>Found a bug, or want a tool that isn't here?</h3>
      <p>Send a note. I usually reply within a few days.</p>
    </div>
    <a href="/contact" class="tk-btn tk-btn--accent" style="flex-shrink:0">Contact →</a>
  </div>
</section>

<%
  // AboutPage + Person structured data. Built as objects so JSON.stringify
  // handles quoting; "<" is escaped so nothing can close the script tag.
  var __person = {
    '@type': 'Person',
    '@id': siteUrl + '/about#george',
    name: 'George Clay',
    url: siteUrl + '/about',
    // TODO(george): add public profile URLs (GitHub, LinkedIn, etc.) — helps
    // search engines tie the byline to you. Left empty rather than guessed.
    sameAs: []
  };
  var __ld = {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    name: title,
    description: metaDescription,
    url: canonicalUrl,
    mainEntity: __person,
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TronKits', url: siteUrl }
  };
%>
<script type="application/ld+json"><%- JSON.stringify(__ld).replace(/</g, '\\u003c') %></script>

<%- include('../partials/footer') %>
